---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
import RockTitle from '../components/RockTitle.astro';
import EarthBand from '../components/EarthBand.astro';
---

<Base title="About Stratum: Multi-Tenancy for Node.js, Born in the MSSP World" description="Stratum grew out of the managed security space, where MSSPs manage MSPs, MSPs manage clients, and almost no tooling handles that hierarchy. It brings proper multi-tenancy to the Node.js ecosystem.">

<Nav />

<main>

<article class="about">
  <!-- Intro -->
  <div class="about-intro">
    <RockTitle lines={['ABOUT']} label="About Stratum" />
    <p class="about-lede">
      Stratum was born in the MSP and MSSP world: years of onboarding clients,
      building monitoring across fragmented platforms, and watching tools fail
      at the one thing they all need, which is real multi-tenancy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Origin story -->
  <div class="about-story">
    <h2>The problem that started it all</h2>
    <p>
      If you have worked in managed security, you know the drill. An MSSP manages MSPs.
      MSPs manage clients. Clients sometimes have sub-clients. That is four levels of
      hierarchy, and almost no tooling handles it correctly.
    </p>

    <!-- The hierarchy as the homepage tenant tree: one rock band per tenant,
         colored by depth and indented under its parent. -->
    <div class="about-hierarchy layer">
      <p class="about-hierarchy-label" id="about-hierarchy-label">The hierarchy Stratum models</p>
      <ol class="inherit-tree" aria-labelledby="about-hierarchy-label">
        <li class="itrow d0"><span class="idepth">d0</span><span class="inode">MSSP</span></li>
        <li class="itrow d1"><span class="idepth">d1</span><span class="inode">MSP</span><span class="imeta">Partner A</span></li>
        <li class="itrow d2"><span class="idepth">d2</span><span class="inode">Client</span><span class="imeta">Acme Corp</span></li>
        <li class="itrow d3"><span class="idepth">d3</span><span class="inode">SubClient</span><span class="imeta">Acme EU</span></li>
        <li class="itrow d1"><span class="idepth">d1</span><span class="inode">MSP</span><span class="imeta">Partner B</span></li>
        <li class="itrow d2"><span class="idepth">d2</span><span class="inode">Client</span><span class="imeta">Globex Inc</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
      Instead of one platform with proper tenant isolation, you end up running
      multiple instances of the same tool. One per MSP, sometimes one per client.
      Monitoring sprawls. Costs balloon. Things get lost between instances. You
      spend more time boxing tooling than actually building solutions for your clients.
    </p>

    <h2>Why Stratum</h2>
    <p>
      Stratum models tenants as a tree. The hierarchy above
      is not a diagram. It is a real data structure in Stratum. Config flows down
      the tree automatically. Isolation strategies apply per tenant. GDPR compliance
      is built in, not bolted on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Node.js had no serious multi-tenancy library. The ecosystem gap was obvious to
      anyone building B2B SaaS or managed services tooling. Stratum fills that gap.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Closing CTA -->
  <div class="about-cta layer">
    <div class="about-cta-install">
      <code>npm install <span class="no-break">@stratum-hq/lib</span> pg</code>
    </div>
    <div class="about-cta-links">
      <a href="https://docs.stratum-hq.org/getting-started/quick-start/" class="btn-primary">Read the docs</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/stratum-hq/Stratum" class="btn-secondary">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>

<!-- Organization structured data -->
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  "url": "https://stratum-hq.org/about",
  "about": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "Stratum HQ",
    "url": "https://stratum-hq.org"
  }
}
</script>

</main>

<EarthBand depth={3} />

<Footer />

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: calc(var(--space-8) * 4) var(--space-6) var(--space-8);
  }

  /* Intro */
  .about-intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .about-intro h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 0.95;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .about-lede {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  /* Story sections */
  .about-story h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .about-story p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Hierarchy diagram callout */
  /* A ledge panel (.layer in global.css). The top padding clears the lip. */
  .about-hierarchy {
    margin: var(--space-8) 0;
    padding: calc(var(--space-6) + 10px) var(--space-6) var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-story .about-hierarchy-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* CTA */
  /* A ledge panel (.layer in global.css). The top padding clears the lip. */
  .about-cta {
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    padding: calc(var(--space-6) + 10px) var(--space-6) var(--space-6);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .about-cta-install {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    background: var(--code-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--code-text);
  }

  .about-cta-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about {
      padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-4);
    }

    .about-hierarchy,
    .about-cta {
      padding-left: var(--space-4);
      padding-right: var(--space-4);
    }
  }
</style>

</Base>
